1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:21,380
In this video I'm going to cover the price
earnings to growth ratio, or PEG ratio as
it's sometimes known, and talk about how
it can be used effectively in the
investment process, and illustrate some
arguments as to why we use it in this way,
using a couple of Excel examples.

2
00:00:21,380 --> 00:00:30,700
So this material is basically
supplementary to video 19, drilling from
the top down 2, where we discuss similar
things with the PE ratio.

3
00:00:30,700 --> 00:00:44,040
So what is it? The PEG ratio is just a
valuation multiple that builds upon the
standard PE ratio, and it aims to improve
upon it by accounting for a company's
anticipated growth in earnings.

4
00:00:44,040 --> 00:01:03,420
Now the reason why this can be regarded as
an improvement comes down to financial
theory, which says we can value something
by discounting its future cash flows, and
to anticipate future cash flows we need to
be able to forecast a growth rate, and
that's the principle behind models like
the DCF model.

5
00:01:03,420 --> 00:01:15,800
So the reason why I'm covering the PEG
ratio in this video is that it's becoming
a much more widely used valuation metric,
and it's important that we as retail
traders know how to deal with it
correctly.

6
00:01:15,800 --> 00:01:24,800
So as far as calculating the PEG ratio
goes, it's fairly simple. It's just the PE
ratio divided by the earnings per share
growth.

7
00:01:24,800 --> 00:01:37,180
So for example, if we have a PE ratio of
10 and annual growth in earnings of 20%,
then the PEG ratio is just 10 divided by
20, which equals 0.5.

8
00:01:37,180 --> 00:01:51,620
Now, theoretical finance teaches us that a
stock with a PEG ratio of less than 1 is
undervalued or cheap, and conversely, a
stock with a PEG of over 1 is overvalued
or expensive.

9
00:01:51,620 --> 00:02:03,500
And sometimes you might see analysts in
the market justify their stock
recommendations in this manner, for
example, by putting a buy recommendation
on a stock with a PEG ratio of less than
1.

10
00:02:03,500 --> 00:02:13,180
But this is the wrong way to deal with the
PEG ratio in the investment process, and
it's the same trap that retail investors
often fall into.

11
00:02:13,180 --> 00:02:30,540
So in reality, the PEG ratio is as
misinterpreted as the PE ratio, and all it
actually represents is the price the
market is willing to buy and sell the
company's earnings and earnings growth
potential for.

12
00:02:31,060 --> 00:02:44,620
So just as in the PE ratio, there's no
cheap or expensive, under or overvalued,
and similarly to the PE ratio, the PEG
ratio can stay the same while the stock
price rises or falls.

13
00:02:44,620 --> 00:02:57,500
So if we take an example of that, stock A
is trading at $10 a share and has a
current earnings per share estimate of $1
and growth in earnings per share estimate
of 20%.

14
00:02:57,500 --> 00:03:06,420
So our PEG ratio there is just the PE
ratio divided by the earnings per share
growth, which is 10 over 20, which is 0.5.

15
00:03:07,060 --> 00:03:19,620
Now, we can also figure out last year's
earnings per share by discounting this
year's expected earnings per share
estimate and the expected earnings per
share growth.

16
00:03:20,100 --> 00:03:33,740
So that's simply obtained by taking the
earnings per share $1 divided by the
discount factor of 1.2, which equates to
the 20% EPS growth figure, and that equals
$0.833.

17
00:03:35,740 --> 00:03:58,240
So now, if we imagine that the company
comes out and guides the market down to a
revised lower EPS number of $0.9, then
this effectively changes the EPS growth
rate to 8%, which we can figure out using
last year's EPS number.

18
00:03:59,560 --> 00:04:12,320
So with the EPS number and EPS growth
figure revised down, all that has to
happen for the PEG ratio to remain
constant at 0.5 is for the price to fall
proportionally.

19
00:04:13,000 --> 00:04:17,680
So in this case, the price just needs to
fall to $3.6.

20
00:04:19,400 --> 00:04:33,080
So as long as the stock price falls
proportionally with the earnings and the
earnings growth relative to the previous
year, then the stock can lose almost all
its value, while the PEG ratio actually
remains the same.

21
00:04:34,760 --> 00:04:45,920
So in this case, theoretical finance would
actually tell us to buy this cheap stock
all the way down from $10 to $3.6, and we
would have lost a lot of money doing so.

22
00:04:47,360 --> 00:04:56,780
Now, in reality, the stock price actually
anticipates moves in EPS and EPS growth
numbers in terms of the PEG ratio.

23
00:04:57,400 --> 00:05:12,760
So for stocks with a PEG ratio of less
than 1, they're actually punished by the
market for continually missing EPS and EPS
growth estimates, and therefore, the
market expects them to miss them again and
again.

24
00:05:13,060 --> 00:05:20,020
And it's prepared to sell the stock at a
low valuation because they regard the
company's earnings a low quality.

25
00:05:20,880 --> 00:05:28,080
And in the future, what's actually likely
to happen is that the EPS and EPS growth
numbers will catch up with the stock
price.

26
00:05:28,760 --> 00:05:36,780
And if you argue with this and actually
buy the stock, you're claiming that
essentially you know more than the market
when you don't.

27
00:05:37,920 --> 00:05:48,940
And similarly, with stocks with a PEG
ratio of over 1, they're rewarded by the
market for continually beating EPS and EPS
growth estimates.

28
00:05:49,080 --> 00:06:01,140
So the market expects them to beat them
again and again and is prepared to buy the
stock, this time at a high valuation,
because the earnings and growth are of
high quality.

29
00:06:02,000 --> 00:06:09,420
And again, in the future, it's likely that
the EPS and EPS growth numbers will
actually catch up to the rising stock
price.

30
00:06:10,080 --> 00:06:24,380
